Bundled certificate authority root
----------------------------------

  Sectigo Public Server Authentication Root R46
  SHA-256 7B:B6:47:A6:2A:EE:AC:88:BF:25:7A:A5:22:D0:1F:FE:A3:95:E0:AB:45:C7:3F:93:F6:56:54:EC:38:F2:5A:06
  app/src/main/res/raw/sectigo_server_root_r46.pem

  A public certificate authority root, published by Sectigo Limited and
  carried by the Mozilla, Microsoft, Apple and Chrome root programmes. It is
  redistributed unmodified so that Android 10 devices, whose trust store
  predates it, can verify api.imf.org and fiscaldata.treasury.gov. It is
  trusted for those two hosts only and in addition to the system trust store,
  never instead of it. A certificate is a public document; redistributing it
  grants this project no authority and creates no trust relationship beyond
  the two hosts named above.

Source policy
-------------

Every source in the stack is reachable without an API key, an account, or a
paid subscription. Each published value carries its source, licence class,
retrieval timestamp and quality tier in the data contract, and that provenance
is rendered next to the value. Attribution is given above; no additional
publication restriction is applied to any value.

Disclaimer
----------

This software produces a statistical description of market conditions. It is
not investment advice, it does not publish a calibrated probability until a
live scoring record exists, and it reports the absence of a source rather than
substituting a guess.

Methods (SPEC v2.1 §26)
-----------------------

The inference layer implements published methods; each is named where it is
used, in the source, in the specification and on the diagnostics screen. No
method is novel and none is attributed to this project.

  Brier, G.W. (1950). Verification of forecasts expressed in terms of
    probability. Monthly Weather Review 78(1).
  Murphy, A.H. (1973). A new vector partition of the probability score.
    Journal of Applied Meteorology 12(4).
  Kalman, R.E. (1960). A new approach to linear filtering and prediction
    problems. Journal of Basic Engineering 82(1).
  Lo, A.W. & MacKinlay, A.C. (1988). Stock market prices do not follow random
    walks: evidence from a simple specification test. Review of Financial
    Studies 1(1).
  Adams, R.P. & MacKay, D.J.C. (2007). Bayesian online changepoint detection.
    arXiv:0710.3742.
  Meucci, A. (2009). Managing diversification. Risk 22(5).
  Vovk, V., Gammerman, A. & Shafer, G. (2005). Algorithmic Learning in a
    Random World. Springer.
  Lei, J., G'Sell, M., Rinaldo, A., Tibshirani, R.J. & Wasserman, L. (2018).
    Distribution-free predictive inference for regression. JASA 113(523).

The split-conformal coverage guarantee assumes exchangeability, which a daily
price series violates. The realised coverage is therefore measured on held-out
sessions and published beside the nominal level rather than claimed.


